Pulmonary Sarcoidosis
Respiratory System
Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, Erythema nodosum, Uveitis, Non-caseating granulomas

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-041
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of painful red nodules on the shins, extreme fatigue, and worsening chronic dry cough requiring urgent evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a weeks-long insidious cough who suddenly developed painful, inflamed skin lesions on their legs and worsening shortness of breath. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute deep vein thrombosis/cellulitis and evaluate for systemic inflammatory disease prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have felt exhausted and been coughing for a month, but today these huge, hot, painful red lumps suddenly broke out all over my shins.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing over 6 weeks with an insidious onset of exertional dyspnea and a chronic, dry, non-productive cough. Over the last 48 hours, the patient reports severe fatigue and the abrupt eruption of painful, erythematous nodules on the anterior shins (erythema nodosum). They describe a vague aching in the large joints (arthralgias) and report feeling generally unwell with unexplained weight loss. The patient also complains of a gritty sensation in both eyes with mild photophobia, suspicious for early uveitis.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted by severe shin pain and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; psychological distress regarding sudden skin eruption.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/78 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 breaths/min | SpO2: 96% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-nourished but fatigued, in obvious discomfort when walking due to lower limb pain.
Physical Exam: Lower extremity examination reveals multiple tender, warm, red nodules on the anterior shins bilaterally, diagnostic of erythema nodosum. Respiratory auscultation is largely clear, with occasional fine inspiratory crackles at the bases. Ophthalmologic exam reveals bilateral conjunctival injection and mild photophobia. The clinical tetrad of these skin findings, eye symptoms, and respiratory complaints is highly suspicious for acute sarcoidosis (Löfgren syndrome variant).
Environmental Observations: Patient has elevated their legs on a chair.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; paracetamol taken with minimal relief for leg pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pulmonary Sarcoidosis - Status: Exacerbated (Löfgren Syndrome presentation)
Differentials:

Bilateral Lower Limb Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The discrete, nodular nature of the lesions strictly on the anterior shins rather than diffuse, confluent erythema, combined with the prominent chronic respiratory symptoms.

Interstitial Lung Disease (IPF) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sudden acute systemic inflammatory signs (erythema nodosum, uveitis) and the patient's relatively young age.

Pulmonary Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of classic erythema nodosum and eye symptoms without the characteristic severe drenching night sweats or purulent hemoptysis, though it must remain a differential until imaging.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic acute manifestations of systemic sarcoidosis, specifically Löfgren syndrome: erythema nodosum, arthralgia, and respiratory symptoms (cough/dyspnea), which typically correlate with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on imaging. The acute, severe pain from the erythema nodosum and the emerging photophobia (uveitis) are causing significant distress and functional limitation. Deferring assessment until the next in-hours period would leave the patient in unmanaged pain and risk progressive ocular inflammation. An urgent after-hours home visit was required to definitively diagnose the skin lesions, rule out deep vein thrombosis or cellulitis, prescribe strong non-steroidal anti-inflammatories, and mandate urgent specialist follow-up for eye involvement and formal pulmonary imaging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral Ibuprofen (600mg three times daily with food) for the arthralgia and erythema nodosum. Generated an urgent referral for a chest X-ray and an immediate ophthalmology review to formally assess for uveitis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear medical-grade graduated compression stockings every morning before getting out of bed; putting these on before gravity pulls fluid down physically supports the inflamed blood vessels in your shins, directly reducing the throbbing pain when you stand.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Compression stockings (Class 1) recommended.
Follow-up: Mandatory optometry/ophthalmology review tomorrow; GP review within 3 days to review chest X-ray results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation (call 000) if vision suddenly blurs or eye pain becomes agonizing, if shortness of breath occurs while sitting perfectly still, or if you begin coughing up blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A formal diagnosis requires a chest X-ray to look for enlarged lymph nodes and eventually a tissue biopsy to confirm non-caseating granulomas.
Clinical Course: The painful lumps on the legs will slowly fade over 2 to 6 weeks like a bruise; the cough and fatigue may take several months to resolve completely.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for assessment immediately if your eyes become extremely sensitive to light or your vision becomes cloudy.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has become overactive, creating tiny clusters of inflammation in your lungs, eyes, and the fat under your skin; the strong anti-inflammatory medication directly shuts down this reaction to stop the pain and swelling.
Actionable Steps: Take the strong ibuprofen strictly with a full meal; swallowing this medication on an empty stomach will directly cause acid burns to your stomach lining, creating a new, severe pain problem.

